WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.468 --> 00:00:09.370
Notice that summer seems longer nowadays?

2
00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:15.153
The average temperature has increased over the past one hundred years, but changes in the earth's climate are

3
00:00:15.193 --> 00:00:15.654
not new.

4
00:00:16.374 --> 00:00:20.137
The earth's climate has changed constantly over its five-billion-year history.

5
00:00:20.857 --> 00:00:25.360
Sometimes, the climate has warmed up so much that the oceans have risen and covered the earth.

6
00:00:26.144 --> 00:00:30.725
These changes are extreme, but they usually occurred gradually over many thousands of years.

7
00:00:31.566 --> 00:00:37.748
The first modern humans inhabited the earth between fifteen thousand and thirty thousand years ago, when most land

8
00:00:37.768 --> 00:00:38.628
was covered in ice.

9
00:00:39.388 --> 00:00:44.870
Fourteen thousand years ago, the last ice sheet began to melt; seven thousand years ago, the ice was

10
00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:48.651
gone. When the "Ice Age" ended, the earth was a different place.

11
00:00:49.211 --> 00:00:51.612
Many kinds of plants and animals had become extinct.

12
00:00:52.468 --> 00:00:56.510
However, the Ice Age was not the last example of extreme climate change.

13
00:00:57.611 --> 00:01:02.714
The Europeans lived through a "Little Ice Age" that began in the fourteenth century and lasted for five

14
00:01:02.734 --> 00:01:03.354
hundred years.

15
00:01:04.175 --> 00:01:09.438
During the Little Ice Age, winters were bitterly cold in many parts of the world, but climate changes

16
00:01:09.498 --> 00:01:12.600
were most thoroughly documented in Europe and North America.

17
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.764
In the mid-seventeenth century, glaciers in the Swiss Alps grew in size, gradually crushing entire villages.

18
00:01:20.524 --> 00:01:25.246
The River Thames in London and the canals of the Netherlands often froze over during the winter.

19
00:01:26.206 --> 00:01:31.689
Sea ice surrounding Iceland extended for miles in every direction, and ships could no longer dock at its

20
00:01:31.709 --> 00:01:36.130
harbors. There were many years of death and famine due to short crop seasons.

21
00:01:36.931 --> 00:01:42.053
In 1850, the world's climate began warming again and the Little Ice Age came to an end.

22
00:01:43.293 --> 00:01:49.376
Over the last century, the earth has undergone severe climate change again—but this time around, it's not getting

23
00:01:49.436 --> 00:01:50.921
colder, it's getting warmer.

24
00:01:51.801 --> 00:01:57.926
The earth has warmed about 1˚Fahrenheit, and the four warmest years of the twentieth century all happened in

25
00:01:57.946 --> 00:01:58.806
the 1990s.

26
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:02.529
This phenomenon is commonly known as "climate change."

27
00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:08.833
Although the earth's climate has drastically altered in the past, the recent climate change is different in two

28
00:02:08.893 --> 00:02:16.238
ways. First, almost all scientists agree that we humans are to blame in some way, and, second, climate

29
00:02:16.258 --> 00:02:18.620
change is occurring at an unprecedented pace.

30
00:02:19.456 --> 00:02:24.760
Climate change and its causes, effects, and solutions have become a major issue for all generations.

31
00:02:25.681 --> 00:02:30.385
In a recent survey in Britain, young people said that global warming was their biggest concern for the

32
00:02:30.425 --> 00:02:34.628
world's future, even more than the threat of terrorism or lack of housing.

33
00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:37.291
Why is the earth's climate changing?

34
00:02:38.011 --> 00:02:42.675
This issue is hotly debated, but most scientists agree that humans are responsible.

35
00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:50.133
The main human activity that negatively affects our climate is the emission of carbon dioxide gas (CO₂) into

36
00:02:50.173 --> 00:02:51.274
the earth's atmosphere.

37
00:02:52.375 --> 00:02:59.421
When power plants burn coal, oil, and natural gas to make electricity, they emit CO₂, and when we

38
00:02:59.501 --> 00:03:04.585
use a car, bus, or plane, they burn gasoline in their engines and emit CO₂.

39
00:03:05.786 --> 00:03:10.170
Too much CO₂in the atmosphere means that the "greenhouse effect" cannot function normally.

40
00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:15.854
The greenhouse effect is a natural process in which sunlight passes through the atmosphere and warms the earth's

41
00:03:15.874 --> 00:03:20.329
surface. In turn, the land and oceans release heat into the atmosphere.

42
00:03:21.049 --> 00:03:26.951
Water vapor, CO₂, and some other gases absorb some of this heat, and the atmosphere warms up.

43
00:03:27.611 --> 00:03:29.291
The rest of the heat goes into space.

44
00:03:30.032 --> 00:03:34.873
If there is too much CO₂ in the atmosphere, too much heat remains in the atmosphere and so

45
00:03:34.913 --> 00:03:36.573
the earth's climate becomes warmer.

46
00:03:37.254 --> 00:03:39.534
And a warmer earth has many consequences.

47
00:03:41.155 --> 00:03:45.676
The effects of climate change are all around us—some are subtle and some are very obvious.

48
00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:53.022
Something that everyone is feeling is hotter weather, called "global warming," with 1998 being the warmest year on

49
00:03:53.082 --> 00:03:57.663
record, and 2002 and 2003 tying for second warmest.

50
00:03:58.743 --> 00:04:02.965
In Europe in 2003, 27,000 people died in heat waves.

51
00:04:03.885 --> 00:04:09.266
Eleven thousand Tuvaluans live on nine coral atolls, totaling ten square miles, east of Australia.

52
00:04:10.127 --> 00:04:15.868
Tuvalu has no industry and burns little petroleum, yet this tiny country is on the front line of

53
00:04:15.969 --> 00:04:16.769
climate change.

54
00:04:17.809 --> 00:04:23.612
Rising sea levels mean that Tuvalu may be completely submerged and then disappear like the lost city of

55
00:04:23.672 --> 00:04:31.855
Atlantis. In 2002, Prime Minister Koloa Talake announced plans to sue the United States and Australia at the

56
00:04:31.915 --> 00:04:37.717
International Court of Justice in the Hague over their disproportionate production of carbon dioxide emissions.

57
00:04:38.818 --> 00:04:43.720
In Antarctica and the Arctic, climate change has reduced the thickness of ice by 40 percent.

58
00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:49.781
Recent changes mean that polar bears may become extinct in this century if weather trends continue.

59
00:04:50.622 --> 00:04:56.223
All kinds of animals and their habits—where they live, what they eat, when they breed—are affected by the

60
00:04:56.263 --> 00:04:56.823
warm weather.

61
00:04:57.704 --> 00:05:00.905
Another major consequence of global warming is extreme weather.

62
00:05:01.445 --> 00:05:04.186
Floods and storms are occurring with increasing intensity.

63
00:05:04.786 --> 00:05:12.468
For example, in 2004, Australia had its worst drought in one hundred years and, during severe tropical storms,

64
00:05:13.032 --> 00:05:16.713
experienced hailstones the size of tennis balls—the biggest in history.

65
00:05:17.813 --> 00:05:24.495
In Canada, data collected from insurance companies showed that over the past thirty years, more natural catastrophes occurred

66
00:05:24.535 --> 00:05:25.876
there than ever before.

67
00:05:26.656 --> 00:05:31.257
With such noticeable changes in the earth's climates, governments have started to take action.

68
00:05:32.118 --> 00:05:37.239
World leaders are slowly acknowledging the existence of climate change and the fact that humans are to blame,

69
00:05:37.879 --> 00:05:39.900
but so far, they have been slow to act.

70
00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.582
The most famous international treaty on global warming is the Kyoto Protocol.

71
00:05:45.522 --> 00:05:52.105
The 141 nations that signed it in 1998 committed to reducing their country's emissions of CO₂.

72
00:05:53.105 --> 00:05:59.508
Notably, the world's largest producer of CO₂, the United States, as well as Australia, refused to sign the

73
00:05:59.548 --> 00:06:02.289
treaty and have received much criticism internationally.

74
00:06:03.189 --> 00:06:07.871
China is the world's second biggest emitter of CO₂ and has signed the Kyoto Protocol.

75
00:06:08.611 --> 00:06:15.420
However, analysts say China faces an uphill battle, with private transport viewed as its major environmental obstacle.

76
00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:20.503
The biggest concern is cars—what will happen when every Chinese citizen drives a car?

77
00:06:21.203 --> 00:06:26.627
What will happen if citizens of developed countries like France and the United States continue to drive cars

78
00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:27.248
all the time?

79
00:06:28.348 --> 00:06:33.892
The good news is that we as individuals can reduce climate change by taking responsibility for our part

80
00:06:33.952 --> 00:06:37.635
in global warming, and subsequently changing our patterns of energy use.

81
00:06:38.275 --> 00:06:40.620
Here are some suggestions: 1.

82
00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:47.483
Tune up your home and make it energy efficient: Heating and cooling our homes, schools, and workplaces use

83
00:06:47.523 --> 00:06:48.643
the most energy of all.

84
00:06:49.184 --> 00:06:51.965
Use insulation and ventilation.

85
00:06:51.985 --> 00:06:58.387
2. Buy wisely: if you buy a new appliance or machine like a computer, washing machine, freezer, or

86
00:06:58.467 --> 00:07:00.848
fridge, choose one that is energy efficient.

87
00:07:01.749 --> 00:07:06.811
3. Leave the car at home—catch public transport, ride a bike, or walk.

88
00:07:07.731 --> 00:07:14.501
4. Grow your own food—the transport, storage, and cooling of food uses an enormous amount of energy.

89
00:07:15.642 --> 00:07:19.306
As American Senator John Kerry remarked, "Climate change is real.

90
00:07:19.647 --> 00:07:22.590
The longer we wait, the harder the problem will be to solve."
